I've been brainstorming around a semi-supervised adaptation setup where I keep a vision foundation model backbone completely frozen and only fine-tune three LoRA experts plus the classifier head. The three experts are a Positive Expert, an Alignment Expert, and a Negative Expert, and the whole point of the design (TriNoL) is to route unlabeled samples to different experts based on pseudo-label confidence rather than randomly, and to give each expert its own tailored loss instead of a shared one.

The routing works like this: I use the weakly augmented unlabeled view to estimate pseudo-label confidence. By default the Positive Expert produces the pseudo-labels since it's trained on labeled data and high-confidence unlabeled samples. For each unlabeled sample $u_i$, I compute $p_i^w=\operatorname{softmax}\big(z^{+}(u_i^w)\big)$, where $z^{+}(u_i^w)$ denotes the logits from the Positive Expert. The pseudo-label and its confidence are $\hat{y}_i=\arg\max_{c} p_{i,c}^w$, $q_i=\max_{c} p_{i,c}^w$.

I use two confidence thresholds, $\tau_{+}$ and $\tau_{-}$, with $0 \leq \tau_{-}<\tau_{+}\leq 1$, and divide unlabeled samples into three confidence regions:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{+} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid q_i \geq \tau_{+}\}, \\
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{a} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid \tau_{-} \leq q_i < \tau_{+}\}, \\
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{-} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid q_i < \tau_{-}\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}$ go to the Positive Expert, samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}$ go to the Alignment Expert, and samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}$ go to the Negative Expert. This routing rule doesn't introduce an extra routing network; it just reuses the same confidence signal that's already present in standard SSL pipelines.

For labeled samples, I anchor adaptation with a supervised loss computed via the Positive Expert:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{sup}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_l|}
 \sum_{(x_i,y_i)\in \mathcal{B}_l}
 \operatorname{CE}
 \big(
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{+}(x_i)), y_i
 \big),
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{CE}(\cdot,\cdot)$ is cross-entropy.

For high-confidence unlabeled samples, the Positive Expert learns from hard pseudo-labels:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pos}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}}
 \operatorname{CE}
 \big(
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{+}(u_i^s)), \hat{y}_i
 \big).
\end{equation}

For medium-confidence unlabeled samples, I don't force a hard pseudo-label decision since these often lie near decision boundaries; instead the Alignment Expert matches the strong-view prediction to the detached weak-view probability:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}}
 \operatorname{KL}
 \big(
 \operatorname{sg}(p_i^w)
 \,\Vert\,
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{a}(u_i^s))
 \big),
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{KL}(\cdot\Vert\cdot)$ is the KL divergence and $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$ is stop-gradient.

For low-confidence unlabeled samples, the Negative Expert is discouraged from assigning high probability to the current top pseudo-label rather than treating it as a clean target:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{neg}}
 =
 -\frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}}
 \log
 \Big(
 1 -
 p_{i,\hat{y}_i}^{-}
 +
 \epsilon
 \Big),
\end{equation}
where $p_i^{-}=\operatorname{softmax}(z^{-}(u_i^s))$, $p_{i,\hat{y}_i}^{-}$ is the probability the Negative Expert assigns to the weak-view top pseudo-label, and $\epsilon$ is a small constant for numerical stability.

The final training objective combines all of these:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}
 =
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{sup}}
 +
 \lambda_{\mathrm{pos}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pos}}
 +
 \lambda_{\mathrm{align}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}
 +
 \lambda_{\mathrm{neg}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{neg}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\mathrm{pos}}$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{align}}$, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{neg}}$ control the strengths of the three unlabeled objectives. In practice $\lambda_{\mathrm{pos}}$ is usually larger since high-confidence pseudo-labels give the most direct supervision, the alignment weight controls how much medium-confidence samples affect the decision boundary, and the negative weight is kept moderate to avoid over-penalizing uncertain samples. Only the LoRA experts and classifier head are updated; the VFM backbone stays frozen throughout.

For my routing ablation, I'm comparing this confidence-based routing scheme against two alternatives: random routing, which assigns unlabeled samples to experts randomly, and shared CE, which uses confidence routing but trains all experts with the same hard pseudo-label loss instead of the expert-specific objectives above. I report mean accuracy over 3 trials where available.

My current baseline results with random routing are: CIFAR-100 N4 accuracy = 80.51%, FOOD-101 N2 accuracy = 87.46%, Semi-Aves accuracy = 61.25%, and ImageNet 1% accuracy = 74.43%.

Note that the random-routing arm retains the expert-specific objectives, while the confidence-routing arm here uses a single shared loss, so two factors differ between the arms.

My question about this setup: after switching from Random Routing (my baseline above) to Confidence Routing + Shared CE, predict the resulting value for each: CIFAR-100 N4 accuracy (in %); FOOD-101 N2 accuracy (in %); Semi-Aves accuracy (in %); ImageNet 1% accuracy (in %).

I've been brainstorming ways to handle the conflict between the reconstruction objective (LCFM) and the perceptual objective (LCPL) when training PCFlow, which uses a latent consistency flow matching backbone for super-resolution. My idea is a conflict-free gradient update, inspired by multi-task gradient surgery but adapted as an asymmetric orthogonal projection: since LCFM provides the structural transport signal and LCPL provides perceptual guidance steering the trajectory toward perceptually realistic regions, whenever the two gradients conflict (i.e. $\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \rangle < 0$), I preserve the perceptual gradient and remove only the component of the structural gradient that conflicts with it. Concretely, denoting $g_{\mathrm{LCFM}} = \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{LCFM}}$ and $g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} = \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{LCPL}}$, the parameter update is:
\begin{equation}
\theta \leftarrow \theta - \eta(\tilde{g}_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t) + \lambda_{\mathrm{LCPL}}(t)g_{\mathrm{LCPL}}(t)),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{g}_{\text{LCFM}}$ is the orthogonal component of $g_{\text{LCFM}}$ with respect to $g_{\text{LCPL}}$:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{g}_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t)
=
g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t)
-
\mathbf{1}_{\{\left\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \right\rangle<0\}}
\frac{\left\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \right\rangle}
{\| g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \|^2}
\, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}}.
\end{equation}
The idea is that the perceptual objective remains an effective steering signal throughout training, while reconstruction updates are incorporated only when they don't interfere with perceptual optimization.

My setup applies this conflict-free gradient alignment from epoch 0, without any preheating period (normally the first 250 epochs are used to optimize only the reconstruction objective with perceptual supervision disabled, before introducing perceptual supervision). Here, instead, I'm comparing this conflict-free alignment applied from the very start against standard joint optimization without gradient alignment, in the same no-preheating setting, on the super-resolution task with the PCFlow latent consistency flow matching backbone.

My baseline results (without gradient alignment) on the super-resolution task are: FID = 46.4, PSNR = 23.32 dB, SSIM = 0.6484, LPIPS = 0.3363.

My question about this setup: after switching from w/o Gradient Alignment (my baseline above) to w/ Gradient Alignment, predict the resulting value for each: super-resolution FID; super-resolution PSNR (in dB); super-resolution SSIM; super-resolution LPIPS.

I've been working on a two-stage pipeline for leukemia cell classification, where Stage 2 performs subtype classification (ALL vs. AML), applied conditionally to the samples predicted as leukemic by Stage 1. For this stage I'm testing three encoders — DinoBloom, BiomedCLIP, and CLIP — cross-dataset trained on 69,400 single-cell images, and evaluated using a held-out dataset protocol on ALL-IDB2 + AML-Cytomorphology (n=3424).

One idea I've been brainstorming to improve this stage is Retrieval-Augmented Classification (RAC). The general concept behind retrieval-augmented methods in AI is to give a pretrained model an external memory retrieval mechanism so it can fetch task-specific information and enhance performance. For VFM-based image classifiers, this means the model doesn't only rely on its pretrained visual representations, but also incorporates additional context to help classify an image more accurately — this is what I'm calling RAC.

The mechanism I've set up consists of a parallel retrieval branch combined with the image encoder. At inference time, the retrieval branch queries pre-encoded images, which provide additional context and are fused with the base classification to create one final classification. Specifically, the RAC module works on the embedding space produced by the frozen or LoRA-adapted encoder. Given a query image, its embedding is compared by cosine similarity against an embedding bank comprising the L2-normalized training embeddings produced by the encoder. The top-$k$ most similar samples make a similarity-weighted vote over classes, forming a retrieval distribution $p_{\text{ret}}$. This distribution is then fused with the linear classifier distribution $p_{\text{probe}}$ in log space:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rac}
\text{logits}_{\text{final}} = \log p_{\text{probe}} + \alpha \log p_{\text{ret}}
\end{equation}

where $\alpha$ weights the contribution of the retrieval branch. For the linear probe configurations, $\alpha$ was selected on an internal validation split derived from the training datasets, searching over the grid $\{0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1\}$. For the LoRA configurations, $\alpha$ was fixed at $0.5$ due to computational budget. The held-out datasets were never used for tuning. The predicted class corresponds to the highest resulting logit, and $k$ was set to 20 in all experiments.

My current baseline, before applying RAC, on the held-out set (ALL-IDB2 + AML-Cytomorphology, n=3424) is as follows, using Linear probe / LoRA configurations without RAC: overall Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903. Broken down by encoder configuration: DinoBloom LoRA gives Accuracy = 0.9617, Recall = 0.4998, F1-score = 0.4902. BiomedCLIP Linear probe gives Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903. BiomedCLIP LoRA gives Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903. CLIP Linear probe gives Accuracy = 0.9118, Recall = 0.485, F1-score = 0.4867. CLIP LoRA gives Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903.

My question about this setup: after switching from Linear probe / LoRA (without RAC) (my baseline above) to Linear probe + RAC / LoRA + RAC, predict the resulting value for each: Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom linear probe Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom linear probe Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom linear probe F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom LoRA Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom LoRA Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom LoRA F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP linear probe Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP linear probe Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP linear probe F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP LoRA Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP LoRA Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP LoRA F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP linear probe Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP linear probe Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP linear probe F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP LoRA Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP LoRA Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP LoRA F1-score (in proportion).

I’ve been iterating on a model-relative predictive setup that asks whether the recent history of a candidate source series contributes out-of-sample information about a target series beyond what is already available from the target’s own recent history. The question is in the Granger sense and does not by itself establish causality. My setup is as follows. Let $x_t$ denote the target series and $y_t$ a candidate source. For a history length $m$, define
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{x}_t=(x_{t-m+1},\ldots,x_t), \qquad
 \mathbf{y}_t=(y_{t-m+1},\ldots,y_t).
\end{equation}
For each forecast horizon $h=1,\ldots,H$, two separate regression branches predict the identical future target $x_{t+h}$. The restricted or baseline branch uses only target history,
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{x}^{(b)}_{t+h}=f_h(\mathbf{x}_t),
 \label{eq:baseline}
\end{equation}
whereas the augmented branch adds the candidate-source history,
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{x}^{(a)}_{t+h}=g_h(\mathbf{x}_t,\mathbf{y}_t).
 \label{eq:augmented}
\end{equation}
The two branches use exactly the same forecast origins, training/test partition, target observations, and prediction engine, so their difference isolates the incremental contribution of source history within the specified model class. The reverse direction is obtained by exchanging $x$ and $y$ and repeating the complete procedure. In the forward “search $\rightarrow$ COVID” direction this means comparing $x\rightarrow x$ with $(x,y)\rightarrow x$; in reverse it means comparing $y\rightarrow y$ with $(y,x)\rightarrow y$.

The final main analysis uses rolling windows of $W=400$ calendar days, target/source history $m=14$ days, maximum forecast horizon $H=14$ days, and a one-day rolling step. A complete window contains
\begin{equation}
 N_{\mathrm{orig}}=W-m-H+1=373
\end{equation}
legal forecast origins that are common to all horizons. Origins are split chronologically: the first 60\% (223 origins) are used for training and the remaining 40\% (150 origins) for held-out evaluation. No random shuffle is used. A one-day rolling step denotes one actual calendar day. Windows containing gaps in the selected source or target are not converted into shorter pseudo-consecutive sequences. Because adjacent complete windows share 399 of 400 calendar days, neighboring rolling estimates are strongly dependent. Percentages of positive windows are therefore interpreted as persistence or temporal coverage descriptors, not as counts of independent replications or as inputs to a binomial significance argument.

I’m running matched LightGBM forecasts over these rolling 400-day windows, horizons 1–14 days, with chronological validation. Eight pre-specified search-indicator pairs (B1–B8) are evaluated in both directions. My baseline branch is the restricted LightGBM forecast using only the target series’ own history. For B2 ‘covid test’ $\rightarrow$ positive rate, the baseline gets 0.014 RMSE (log scale) and 0.559 FEDE at $h=1$, 0.01659 RMSE (log scale) and 0.695 FEDE at $h=7$, and 0.02104 RMSE (log scale) and 0.769 FEDE at $h=14$. For B3 ‘loss of smell’ $\rightarrow$ cases, the baseline gets 0.702 FEDE at $h=1$, 0.813 FEDE at $h=7$, and 0.878 FEDE at $h=14$. For B7 ‘lockdown’ $\rightarrow$ stringency, the baseline gets 8.997 RMSE (stringency index) and 0.689 FEDE at $h=1$, 10.103 RMSE (stringency index) and 0.702 FEDE at $h=7$, and 12.112 RMSE (stringency index) and 0.744 FEDE at $h=14$.

My question about this setup: after switching from Restricted/baseline branch: LightGBM forecast using only the target series' own history (my baseline above) to Augmented branch: LightGBM forecast using target history plus source-series history, predict the resulting value for each: B2 'covid test' -> positive rate, h=1 RMSE baseline -> augmented (in RMSE (log scale)); B2 'covid test' -> positive rate, h=1 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B2 'covid test' -> positive rate, h=7 RMSE baseline -> augmented (in RMSE (log scale)); B2 'covid test' -> positive rate, h=7 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B2 'covid test' -> positive rate, h=14 RMSE baseline -> augmented (in RMSE (log scale)); B2 'covid test' -> positive rate, h=14 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B3 'loss of smell' -> cases, h=1 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B3 'loss of smell' -> cases, h=7 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B3 'loss of smell' -> cases, h=14 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B7 'lockdown' -> stringency, h=1 RMSE baseline -> augmented (in RMSE (stringency index)); B7 'lockdown' -> stringency, h=1 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B7 'lockdown' -> stringency, h=7 RMSE baseline -> augmented (in RMSE (stringency index)); B7 'lockdown' -> stringency, h=7 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE); B7 'lockdown' -> stringency, h=14 RMSE baseline -> augmented (in RMSE (stringency index)); B7 'lockdown' -> stringency, h=14 FEDE baseline -> augmented (in FEDE).

I’ve been working on auxiliary reward shaping for the BIRD-RL training curriculum, specifically looking at whether denser signals can help when the execution-based reward alone gives no positive feedback. One of the designs I’m trying is a heuristic rubric-based reward. The idea is to add a small shaping signal only for trajectories whose execution test pass rate is zero; any trajectory that passes at least one test case is still optimized primarily by the execution-based reward.

My setup is this: the rubric-based reward has two criteria. The first is whether the submitted SQL executes successfully. The second is whether the model identifies known bugs in its reasoning. For the bug-identification part, I match the model’s reasoning against a pre-extracted bug checklist using an LLM verifier. The reward is computed as

\begin{equation}
R_{\mathrm{rubric}}
=
0.1 \times \mathbb{I}_{\mathrm{exec}}
+
0.1 \times
\frac{N_{\mathrm{bug}}^{\mathrm{id}}}{N_{\mathrm{bug}}}.
\end{equation}

Here, \(\mathbb{I}_{\mathrm{exec}}\) indicates whether the submitted SQL executes without syntax errors, \(N_{\mathrm{bug}}\) is the number of known bugs in the checklist, and \(N_{\mathrm{bug}}^{\mathrm{id}}\) is the number of bugs identified by the model. The prompts for bug extraction and verification are the ones I have in Figure \ref{fig:rubric_extract_prompt} and \ref{fig:rubric_verify_prompt}.

Right now, with BIRD-Talon-7B using only the execution-based reward, my baselines on BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite are: Quer. Success Rate 46.13%, Mana. Success Rate 45.33%, Pers. Success Rate 40.43%, and Overall Success Rate 44.4%.

My question about this setup: after switching from BIRD-Talon-7B (execution-based reward only) (my baseline above) to (f) w/ Heuristic Rubric, predict the resulting value for each: BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Quer.) Success Rate (SR%) (in %); BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Mana.) Success Rate (SR%) (in %); BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Pers.) Success Rate (SR%) (in %); BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Overall) Success Rate (SR%) (in %).
